Q1: Why is it difficult to appeal to academic writers about OA policies? 
  A. They are hard to capture the attention of 
  B. All of the other answers are correct
  C. They work too hard to be concerned with publishing intricacies 
  D. They are not a homogenous group 
  Gold answer: B (2)

Q2: Why are funding agencies and universities concerned with OA policy?
  A. They are seeking to limit the power that private publishers have 
  B. They want to ensure researchers are able to work in the most effective way possible 
  C. They want to influence the content of the authors’ works 
  D. They are looking to maximize their profits 
  Gold answer: B (2)

Q3: Why are there no gold OA mandates?
  A. They would not be effective as they would deter authors from submitting to journals with Gold OA mandates 
  B. OA mandates have not become popular in the academic field yet 
  C. They are illegal and no publishers would risk breaking the law 
  D. They are not needed as most authors only submit work to one journal 
  Gold answer: A (1)

Q4: Which of the OA mandates gives the author the most control over their work? 
  A. Libre green mandates 
  B. Loophole mandates
  C. Deposit mandates
  D. Rights-retention mandates 
  Gold answer: D (4)

Q5: What is the main difference between funding groups and academic institutions when it comes to OA?
  A. Funding groups allow waivers for the authors to not release their work 
  B. Funding groups only allow Gold OA policies 
  C. Academic institutions only allow Gold policies 
  D. Funding groups do not allow waivers for the authors to not release their work 
  Gold answer: D (4)

Q6: In which situations does truly unconditional OA policy apply?
  A. When publishing work in a journal 
  B. When working for a university 
  C. There are no situations where unconditional OA applies 
  D. When working in the field of Physics
  Gold answer: C (3)

Q7: Why are green gratis mandates spreading faster than green libre mandates 
  A. Gold mandates are more popular than libre green mandates 
  B. University resistance to libre green mandates 
  C. Author resistance to libre green mandates 
  D. Publisher resistance to libre green mandates 
  Gold answer: D (4)

Q8: How does the author suggest that the transition will be made to more liberal OA policies? 
  A. By researchers demanding more OA to further their work 
  B. By more academic and funding institutions adoptions OA policies
  C. By education faculty about the benefits of OA policies 
  D. By more publishers willingly adopting OA policies
  Gold answer: B (2)

Q9: What seems to be the greatest challenge involved with getting authors involved in helping to create OA policies?
  A. They can't be bothered by such mundane information.
  B. They do not believe it is something they are responsible for.
  C. They do not have a stake in the process.
  D. Their attention is focused elsewhere.
  Gold answer: D (4)

Q10: The irony in authors not taking a more proactive role in decisions regarding OA policies is 
  A. though they are scholars, they are not competent enough to understand the processes involved.
  B. they leave their fate to others.
  C. they leave their fate to chance.
  D. they ultimately hold the power behind the decisions.
  Gold answer: D (4)

Q11: Caution must be taken with decisions surrounding OA policies because
  A. those seeking information are ultimately the ones who will suffer if the wrong decisions are made.
  B. institutions will make the policies that are best for them, not others involved.
  C. there is not enough information available concerning the long-term effects of OA policies.
  D. certain mandates will limit where authors can publish their works.
  Gold answer: D (4)

Q12: When concerning green OA and gold OA,
  A. there are many areas that remain unclear, thus causing policy-making to be difficult unless a greater understanding of their distinctions is garnered.
  B. all OA mandates are gold, but this is often misconstrued.
  C. mandates for gold OA are the only ones that make sense.
  D. there is really no difference.
  Gold answer: A (1)

Q13: The verbiage used for these policies is
  A. cannot be misconstrued.
  B. is easily misconstrued.
  C. considered gold-standard.
  D. is agreed upon universally.
  Gold answer: B (2)

Q14: The issue with using more accurate phrasing to describe OA policies is 
  A. the accurate phrasing doesn't seem to exist and needs to be created.
  B. one simple word will always trump longer, detailed phrasing.
  C. no one takes the time to read long pieces of text, so it will be lost on the reader anyway.
  D. the need for one word to replace the more accurate phrasing is often required, and that word may not be the "right" term, but it's the closest fit available.
  Gold answer: D (4)

Q15: Who seems to be taking advantage of the diction used in OA policies and why?
  A. Researchers because they are able to hide behind the wording of the policies in order to stay out of controversies.
  B. Publishers because they can use the diction as a scare tactic to those looking to publish their work.
  C. Institutions because they can skew the policies to their advantage.
  D. University faculty members because they can use the diction in the policies to continue to maintain control of how those policies are perceived.
  Gold answer: B (2)

Q16: Every time a strong OA policy is put into use, 
  A. the system is strengthened, making future policies and decisions easier to develop.
  B. researchers pull further away from the want to publish their work.
  C. the prior structure is weakened, eventually leading to the end of OA.
  D. universities can charge higher fees to access the information.
  Gold answer: A (1)

Q17: One aspect of having university faculty members vote on these policies
  A. it is taking the power from the publishers.
  B. it shows the futility of the system.
  C. has been almost unbelievable, as many of those votes were unanimous.  
  D. OA policies are sure to strengthen because the greatest minds in the world are behind the decision-making process.
  Gold answer: C (3)

Q18: Why are publishers so reluctant to get on board with these OA policies?
  A. They prefer to leave those decisions to others.
  B. They are afraid that they are going to lose funding.
  C. They fear that they will lose their control over those seeing to have their works published.
  D. They do not believe that they play any role in the OA policies.
  Gold answer: C (3)
